{
 "manufacturer": "Invertek Drives",
 "manufacturer_slug": "invertek-drives",
 "model": "Optidrive Elevator Core (built-in Modbus RTU)",
 "model_slug": "optidrive-elevator-core",
 "short_model": "Elevator Core",
 "aliases": [
  "Elevator Core",
  "Optidrive Elevator Core"
 ],
 "equipment_type": "Variable frequency drive",
 "applies_to": "Invertek Elevator Core lift drives on the RJ45 Modbus RTU port, per the online Elevator Core user guide (support.invertekdrives.com).",
 "summary": "The Elevator Core is Invertek's drive for lift (elevator) motors, geared or gearless, open or closed loop. Modbus RTU is built in on the RJ45 port. Register 1 runs the drive forward or reverse and resets faults, register 2 sets the speed in the drive's active unit, and registers 5-48 report status, speed, current, torque, power, faults and run time. Parameters sit at their group-and-number register, and group 0 monitor values at 10000 + number.",
 "transport": [
  "RTU"
 ],
 "defaults": {
  "unit_id": 1,
  "baud": 115200,
  "parity": "None",
  "data_bits": null,
  "stop_bits": 1,
  "tcp_port": null
 },
 "addressing": {
  "convention": "1-based",
  "note": "Registers are numbered from 1. The guide's worked telegrams settle it: the control-word write is broken down as physical address 0x0000 = logical register 1, and the status-word read uses physical 0x0005 for register 6. Parameters use group and number (P1-01 = register 101 = wire address 100); group 0 is read at 10000 + parameter number."
 },
 "default_word_order": null,
 "firmware": null,
 "source": {
  "title": "Elevator Core User Guide (online) - Modbus RTU Individual Register Details",
  "doc_number": null,
  "revision_date": null,
  "url": "https://support.invertekdrives.com/elevator-core/en/modbus-rtu-individual-register-details.html",
  "pages": "Topics: Modbus RTU Individual Register Details (control word, speed reference, status word, control and monitoring registers); Modbus RTU Specification (modbus-rtu-specification.html); Modbus RTU Connecting to the Drive (modbus-rtu-connecting-to-the-drive.html); Group 2 parameters (group-2-parameter-descriptions.html)"
 },
 "notes": [
  "Source is Invertek's official online user guide (HTML), spread over the topics named in the source pages field.",
  "Fault reset: the bit tables put reset on control-word bit 2, while one troubleshooting line says bit 3; this map follows the bit tables (bit 2, also shown in P2-50). The reset only acts on a 0 to 1 change.",
  "A troubleshooting line says the fault code is in the upper byte of register 6, but the status-word topic defines all 16 bits of register 6 and puts the fault code in P2-56 (register 256) and register 18. This map follows the status-word topic.",
  "P2-56 low-byte status bits are left as a plain value: the register-details topic and the group 2 topic give different bit layouts.",
  "Speed registers (2, 5, 7, 22) use the drive's active unit, set by P4-06 and P3-08: Hz (0.1), rpm (1), ft/s (0.01) or m/s (0.01). They are kept raw. Signedness is not printed; speeds are typed INT16 because the drive runs in both directions.",
  "Registers 3, 4, 12, 32, 33 and 40-47 are reserved. The CANopen interface and the full parameter list are not covered here.",
  "Supported functions: 03, 06 and 16."
 ],
